<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="localbox"><p class="localbox-t">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Large hail</li><li>Straight-line wind</li><li>Blizzards</li><li>Grass fires</li></ul><p class="localbox-n"><b>Areas we see:</b> Older blocks near downtown, boom-era subdivisions on the north and west edges, apartment complexes, and manufactured home communities</p></div>
<h2>Insurance appraisal in Williston</h2>
<p>Williston is the seat of Williams County, near where the Missouri River and Yellowstone River meet in the far northwest corner of the state. For most of its history it was a modest farm and rail town. During the 2010s Bakken oil boom, it grew faster than almost anywhere else in the state. Apartment complexes, townhomes, single-family subdivisions, manufactured home communities and workforce housing went up quickly on the edges of town, around an older core of frame houses and midcentury ranches near downtown.</p>
<h3>Weather in the northwest corner</h3>
<p>Williston’s weather is harsh and dry. Summer thunderstorms, mostly from May through August, bring large hail and straight-line winds that can damage roofs, siding and vehicles across whole neighborhoods. Winters bring blizzards, drifting snow and stretches of severe cold. In dry years, grass fires are a real risk on the edges of town and in rural Williams County. Each of these perils has a different effect on the housing types built during the boom.</p>
<h3>How boom-era construction shows up in claims</h3>
<p>Rapid growth meant a lot of building in a short time, often by crews from outside the region. That history affects how claims play out today.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Large multifamily roofs.</strong> Apartment and townhome buildings have big roof areas, so small measurement errors, missed waste factors or skipped steep charges can add up quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Matching across buildings and units.</strong> Boom-era complexes often used the same siding and shingle colors across many buildings. Repairing one section with a different product can leave an obvious mismatch.</li>
<li><strong>Manufactured homes.</strong> Metal roofs, skirting, vinyl siding and windows on manufactured homes are often priced with general rates that do not reflect specialized materials or labor.</li>
<li><strong>Code-related items.</strong> When damaged components are replaced, work that local building requirements may call for is sometimes left off estimates. Whether it is covered depends on the policy’s ordinance or law provisions.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze damage.</strong> Pipes in exterior walls or poorly insulated spaces can freeze during long cold snaps, leading to water damage in walls, floors and lower levels.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Where appraisal fits</h3>
<p>Appraisal is a way to resolve a disagreement about the amount of a covered loss. Each side selects an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and an agreement signed by two of the three usually sets the amount. It does not decide coverage, and it is not a lawsuit. If the dispute is really about whether a peril is excluded, we will say so plainly.</p>
<p>North Dakota Insurance Appraiser reviews the policy and estimate, documents the damage and prepares an independent line-by-line valuation using current pricing in the Williston market, which can differ from eastern North Dakota. If appraisal makes sense, we can act as your appraiser. We do not promise results.</p>
<p>Homeowners, landlords and property managers in Williston can call <strong>+1 (844) 537-1427</strong> to talk about a claim.</p>
